# `__modname__` business module

`weld add module __modname__` generated this package. weld writes it once and
records every file in `weld.json`; it does not regenerate them, so your edits
survive later `weld add` commands.

## What it is

An HTTP business module wired under `GET /api/__modname__`:

- `dto.go` — the typed `Request` and `Response`.
- `service.go` — the `Service` interface and the default `NewService`.
- `module.go` — `Register` and the HTTP handler.

The handler writes through `internal/httpx`, the shared toolkit: `httpx.JSON`
wraps a value in the `{code, msg, data}` envelope and `httpx.Error` writes an
error envelope with `data: null`. Use `httpx.RawText`, `httpx.Bytes` or
`httpx.NoContent` for a response that must stay unwrapped.

## Replaceable example, not persistence

The default `Service` is an in-memory development demo. It writes nothing to
disk and keeps no state across restarts. Replace the body of `NewService` (or
return your own implementation) to add real behaviour; keep the `Service`
interface and the `NewService` symbol stable, because both wirings call them.

## How it is wired

- Without Loom, `internal/httpserver/__modname___route.go` builds the service
  and registers the route through the `weld:routes` extension point.
- With Loom, the generated `internal/di` graph provides
  `__modname__.NewService` and registers the module on the composed mux.

To change the wire format or add endpoints, edit this package; the route prefix
and registration live in `module.go`.
